posts.body:
This is very old, but I'm answering because maybe someone is developing a game today and has a similar question.
I've dealt with the Game_Interpreter and it seems to have somewhat complex logic. It's easy to wait for a message, but not for an animation. So you have to implement this waiting yourself.
What I'm saying requires you to understand a little bit of JavaScript and create your own plugin, but don't be scared, in essence creating a plugin is just saving a JavaScript file (.js) in the plugins folder, activating it in RPG Maker and that's all, with that the code written in it will be executed.
All
Game_Characers
(events, the player, etc.) have an
update
function that is called 60 times per second (once per frame), and they also have the boolean operation
isAnimationPlaying
, which indicates whether that character is currently running an animation or not. You must override the
update
option so that when the animation in question is running, the other animation is not launched.
You can simply query
isAnimationPlaying
and when it returns false then you use
requestAnimation
on the event you want the next animation to play.
It could be optimized a little more, since adding it to update will cause the check to be done throughout the game, even when you are no longer interested in the matter, but it is a way.
In the following code I override the
update
operation of
Game_Map
instead of
Game_Character
. This is because the map will update 60 times per second, but if I were to override
update
in
Game_Character
, this would be done 60 times per second for each event existing on the map, and doing all this checking every time for each event is nonsense. Even this code could be improved further, but I tried to make it clear rather than optimal.
This must be tested in a .js file on plugin folder, not in a script call.
JavaScript:
//Reference to the original update function
var firstAnimationDone = false, secondAnimationDone = false;
var firstAnimationStart = false, secondAnimationStart = false;
var _Game_Map_update = Game_Map.prototype.update;
Game_Map.prototype.update = function(sceneActive) {
    //Call normal map update function
    _Game_Map_update.call(this,sceneAtive);

    //Check animation. In this example the animation is over the player ($gamePlayer).
    //We must to know the ID of animations to run, in this example the first animation
    //is the numnber 1 and the second is the number 2. The animation ID can be shown
    //in RPG Maker animations on Data Base.
    if(firstAnimationDone==false){
        $gamePlayer.requestAnimation(1); //Start first animation.
        firstAnimationStart = true;
    }

    //Check if first animation is done.
    if(!$gamePlayer.isAnimationPlaying() && firstAnimationStart){
        firstAnimationDone = true;
    }//Check if second animation is done.
    else if(!gamePlayer.isAnimationPlaying() && secondAnimationStart){
        secondAnimationDone = true;
    }

    //Check if first animation has done and if we must start the second.
    if(firstAnimationDone && !secondAnimationStart){
        $gamePlayer.requestAnimation(2); //Start second animation.
        secondAnimationStart;
    }
}
